( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.01', 'Labour as a Factor of Production', 'Analyze how businesses classify human resources among the factors of production used to create goods and services. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Managers who coordinate schedules supply labour as human resources alongside coordination duties.', 'Insurance claims handlers processing policies supply labour in a service firm.', 'Labour is restricted to manual shop-floor work and excludes planners or accountants.', 'Installing software is a want rather than labour because the service is intangible.', 'Seasonal pickers fall outside labour because their employment lasts only weeks.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — This statement is correct. Managers who coordinate schedules supply labour as human resources alongside coordination duties.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, management deploys human resources even when it also involves entrepreneurship. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. Insurance claims handlers processing policies supply labour in a service firm.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, office and service staff are human resources classified as labour. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Labour is restricted to manual shop-floor work and excludes planners or accountants.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

Applied to this claim, labour includes all human resources, not only physical manual tasks. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Installing software is a want rather than labour because the service is intangible.

The section on factors of production and how businesses combine them frequently contrasts two similar ideas side by side. A comparison statement is true only if the relationship is stated in the right direction and applies to the right concept pair.

Applied to this claim, skilled intangible work is still labour as human resources. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Students often remember that two concepts differ but swap the direction of the comparison. Verify which side of the pair is longer, larger, riskier, or more regulated.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Seasonal pickers fall outside labour because their employment lasts only weeks.

The scenario is a worked example of factors of production and how businesses combine them. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

Applied to this claim, paid seasonal workers still supply human resources classified as labour. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.'], '3/5', 1, 'full' ),
( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.02', 'Styrian Winemaker Harvest Season', 'Consider a wine-growing business that uses hillside vineyards, seasonal pickers, leased bottling equipment, and specialist fermentation know-how. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['The hillside vineyards represent the land factor as natural resources.', 'Seasonal pickers and the cellar master both supply labour for the winery.', 'The leased bottling line counts as capital even though the winery does not own it.', 'Fermentation knowledge applied to blending draws on knowledge as a production factor.', 'Barrel orders are capital decisions, so entrepreneurship is absent.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, false], ARRAY['TRUE — The claim is correct. The hillside vineyards represent the land factor as natural resources.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, vineyards are natural resources and therefore part of land. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. Seasonal pickers and the cellar master both supply labour for the winery.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, all human resources in production, seasonal or permanent, are labour. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. The leased bottling line counts as capital even though the winery does not own it.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, productive equipment used in operations is capital whether owned or leased. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. Fermentation knowledge applied to blending draws on knowledge as a production factor.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, production knowledge and experience are important factors in winemaking. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Barrel orders are capital decisions, so entrepreneurship is absent.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

Applied to this claim, coordinating factors and contracts is entrepreneurship. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.'], '3/5', 2, 'full' ),
( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.03', 'Land and Natural Resources', 'Analyze what the land factor includes in production. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['River water used to irrigate crops represents a natural-resource land input.', 'Timber ready for milling is land because it originates from trees.', 'Mineral rights are capital because they can be bought and sold.', 'Oak barrels are land because oak comes from forests.', 'Land refers only to fenced factory sites and excludes forests or fisheries.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — The claim is correct. River water used to irrigate crops represents a natural-resource land input.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, water sources used in production are natural resources within land. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Timber ready for milling is land because it originates from trees.

The scenario is a worked example of factors of production and how businesses combine them. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

Applied to this claim, processed logs for milling are inputs; standing forests are land. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. Mineral rights are capital because they can be bought and sold.

The scenario is a worked example of factors of production and how businesses combine them. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

Applied to this claim, unextracted minerals are natural resources within land. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Oak barrels are land because oak comes from forests.

Here you must apply ideas from factors of production and how businesses combine them to a concrete situation rather than recite a definition from memory. Ownership, customer type, stakeholder group, or country of production usually decides the verdict.

The statement overreaches because processed barrels used in production are capital. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Land refers only to fenced factory sites and excludes forests or fisheries.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

Applied to this claim, land includes fisheries, forests, and minerals as natural resources. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.'], '2/5', 3, 'full' ),
( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.04', 'Capital in Production', 'Review statements about capital as a factor of production. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Leased diagnostic tools used by technicians still function as capital.', 'Spare parts inventory kept for same-day repairs is capital supporting service delivery.', 'Machinery, plant, vehicles, and financial resources used in operations are capital.', 'Cash reserves held to finance payroll between invoice cycles form part of capital.', 'A fleet of delivery vans used to ship finished goods is capital.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, true, true], ARRAY['TRUE — This statement is correct. Leased diagnostic tools used by technicians still function as capital.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, leased equipment used in production is capital. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — This statement is correct. Spare parts inventory kept for same-day repairs is capital supporting service delivery.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, inventories used to deliver services belong to capital. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

