The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, capital includes equipment and financial resources applied to production. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. Cash reserves held to finance payroll between invoice cycles form part of capital.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, financial resources used to operate the business are capital. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. A fleet of delivery vans used to ship finished goods is capital.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, vehicles used in production and distribution are capital equipment. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.'], '3/5', 4, 'full' ),
( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.05', 'Factory Production Shift', 'Consider a manufacturing plant running a shift while sourcing materials and scheduling engineers. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Production tools and testing equipment on the line are capital in secondary production.', 'Engineers monitoring product quality supply specialised labour.', 'Plant management coordinating materials, staff, and orders supplies entrepreneurship.', 'Materials are land because copper is extracted from the earth.', 'Knowledge is a consumer good unless the firm registers patents.'], ARRAY[true, true, true, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. Production tools and testing equipment on the line are capital in secondary production.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, manufacturing equipment is part of the capital factor. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is correct. Engineers monitoring product quality supply specialised labour.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, engineering and inspection work is skilled human resource use. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'TRUE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is correct. Plant management coordinating materials, staff, and orders supplies entrepreneurship.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, coordinating production factors and timing is entrepreneurship. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. Materials are land because copper is extracted from the earth.

Here you must apply ideas from factors of production and how businesses combine them to a concrete situation rather than recite a definition from memory. Ownership, customer type, stakeholder group, or country of production usually decides the verdict.

The statement overreaches because raw and processed inputs are materials combined with other factors; finished goods are outputs. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Knowledge is a consumer good unless the firm registers patents.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

The statement overreaches because knowledge applied in production is a recognised factor. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.'], '3/5', 5, 'full' ),
( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.06', 'Entrepreneurship as a Factor of Production', 'Review entrepreneurship among the factors of production. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Entrepreneurship brings land, labour, and capital together to organise production.', 'Uncertainty is borne by lenders alone, not by entrepreneurs.', 'Financial resources are entrepreneurship because both involve business decisions.', 'Hiring staff converts all coordination into labour with no entrepreneurship left.', 'Selecting equipment is capital allocation only, so entrepreneurship is absent.'], ARRAY[true, false, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. Entrepreneurship brings land, labour, and capital together to organise production.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, entrepreneurship coordinates the other factors and bears organisational responsibility. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

No qualifying word breaks the definition or scenario, so the answer is true.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. Uncertainty is borne by lenders alone, not by entrepreneurs.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

The statement overreaches because owners and organisers bear business uncertainty as entrepreneurship. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Financial resources are entrepreneurship because both involve business decisions.

The scenario is a worked example of factors of production and how businesses combine them. Translate the story into the textbook category first; only then judge whether the sentence describes that category accurately.

Applied to this claim, finance is capital; entrepreneurship coordinates rather than replaces it. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. Hiring staff converts all coordination into labour with no entrepreneurship left.

Although the subject matter is factors of production and how businesses combine them, this is really a logic check on overstatement. Non-profit surpluses, mixed economies, and partial market failure are classic cases where absolute wording fails.

Applied to this claim, coordination and risk-bearing remain entrepreneurship with employees present. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Watch the absolute wording "all": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'FALSE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is false. Selecting equipment is capital allocation only, so entrepreneurship is absent.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

The statement overreaches because choosing and organising inputs reflects entrepreneurship. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

The statement sounds plausible but fails on precision, so it is false.'], '2/5', 6, 'full' ),
