( '3.1', 'CASE 3.1.09', 'Combining Production Factors', 'Analyze how businesses combine factors to create offerings. Evaluate the following economic assertions:', ARRAY['Service firms combine labour with capital, technology, and entrepreneurial coordination.', 'A business combines factors of production to offer goods and/or services to customers.', 'Whichever factor is dominant replaces the need for any other factor.', 'Only manufacturing combines factors; services use labour alone.', 'Automated lines remove labour and knowledge from manufacturing entirely.'], ARRAY[true, true, false, false, false], ARRAY['TRUE — Evaluated against the textbook standard, this assertion is correct. Service firms combine labour with capital, technology, and entrepreneurial coordination.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

Applied carefully, service delivery integrates multiple factors, not labour alone. Nothing in the sentence stretches the concept beyond its standard use.

Every part of the claim aligns with the standard concept, so the statement stands.', 'TRUE — The claim is correct. A business combines factors of production to offer goods and/or services to customers.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

In this setting, businesses exist by combining resources to deliver goods or services. The wording matches the textbook relationship without adding extra conditions.

The reasoning chain is complete, so mark the statement true.', 'FALSE — This statement is false. Whichever factor is dominant replaces the need for any other factor.

The relevant theory comes from factors of production and how businesses combine them. Identify which definition or relationship the sentence is trying to test before deciding true or false.

The statement overreaches because one dominant factor does not remove the need to combine others. The trap is to agree with the topic while missing the one detail that breaks the logic.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.', 'FALSE — On the Fuhrmann definition used in the entrance exam, the sentence is false. Only manufacturing combines factors; services use labour alone.

This statement draws on factors of production and how businesses combine them. Treat it as a checklist against the core concept named in the stem, not as a general opinion about business.

Applied to this claim, service and manufacturing firms both combine multiple factors. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Because the decisive detail is wrong, mark the statement false.', 'FALSE — The claim is false. Automated lines remove labour and knowledge from manufacturing entirely.

Although the subject matter is factors of production and how businesses combine them, this is really a logic check on overstatement. Non-profit surpluses, mixed economies, and partial market failure are classic cases where absolute wording fails.

Applied to this claim, manufacturing still uses labour, knowledge, and coordination alongside capital. That is why the sentence does not survive careful reading.

Watch the absolute wording "entirely": exam statements often sound plausible until a single scope word turns an otherwise familiar idea into an overclaim.

Once the overclaim or mislabel is exposed, the only consistent answer is false.'], '3/5', 9, 'full' ),
